.{
    // This is the default name used by packages depending on this one. For
    // example, when a user runs `zig fetch --save <url>`, this field is used
    // as the key in the `dependencies` table. Although the user can choose a
    // different name, most users will stick with this provided value.
    //
    // It is redundant to include "zig" in this name because it is already
    // within the Zig package namespace.
    .name = .zoxy,
    // This is a [Semantic Version](https://semver.org/).
    // In a future version of Zig it will be used for package deduplication.
    .version = "0.9.0",
    // Together with name, this represents a globally unique package
    // identifier. This field is generated by the Zig toolchain when the
    // package is first created, and then *never changes*. This allows
    // unambiguous detection of one package being an updated version of
    // another.
    //
    // When forking a Zig project, this id should be regenerated (delete the
    // field and run `zig build`) if the upstream project is still maintained.
    // Otherwise, the fork is *hostile*, attempting to take control over the
    // original project's identity. Thus it is recommended to leave the comment
    // on the following line intact, so that it shows up in code reviews that
    // modify the field.
    .fingerprint = 0xa872299f36b5e445, // Changing this has security and trust implications.
    // Tracks the earliest Zig version that the package considers to be a
    // supported use case.
    .minimum_zig_version = "0.17.0",
    // This field is optional.
    // Each dependency must either provide a `url` and `hash`, or a `path`.
    // `zig build --fetch` can be used to fetch all dependencies of a package, recursively.
    // Once all dependencies are fetched, `zig build` no longer requires
    // internet connectivity.
    .dependencies = .{
        // Audited fork: zoxy-io/libxev branch zoxy-zig-0.17 = upstream
        // 451a2bc03644c2b70fb28a808313d12989bfa8e3 (upstream's Zig 0.17
        // port of the audited snapshot 9ce8e8e) plus six commits, each its
        // own stacked PR, replayed onto it unchanged — audited as the left
        // SHA, pinned as the right:
        //   c369817 66867ab expose io_uring setup flags through Options
        //   6bd950d e0ec7a0 expose the CQ depth (IORING_SETUP_CQSIZE) —
        //                   the c10k lever
        //   b3d6b55 b87aed5 keep the raw errno on the Completion
        //                   (zoxy-io/libxev#2)
        //   24da6d0 9151a66 recv_group — provided-buffer receives
        //                   (zoxy-io/libxev#3)
        //   fe5a07b 813b4b0 leave readiness to the wait in submit (kqueue)
        //   010be2a b0268b1 let connect address an AF_UNIX pathname socket
        //                   (zoxy-io/libxev#5, #303)
        // Diff against that upstream commit to audit; the pin moves only
        // after re-audit.
        //
        // Re-audit for b0268b1 (Zig 0.17), 2026-11-13. Two halves, and
        // neither changes what a zoxy build runs.
        //
        // The base moved 9ce8e8e -> 451a2bc, which is upstream's 0.17 port
        // (mitchellh/libxev#249: 2c916b8 build.zig, 819ffa1 the tree) and
        // nothing else — 9ce8e8e is its parent. Read in full: `**` becomes
        // `@splat` in tests, `@typeInfo(...).decls` becomes `decl_names` in
        // two comptime API checks, dynamic.zig follows one std type rename,
        // and a test buffer is filled by a loop. No backend, op, error
        // mapping or `Completion` field is touched.
        //
        // The six commits replayed without a conflict, and
        // `git range-diff 9ce8e8e..010be2a 451a2bc..b0268b1` marks every one
        // `=` — patch-identical to what the records below audited. Checked
        // under 0.17.1: libxev's own suite (147 pass, 4 skip) in Debug and
        // ReleaseSafe on Linux, and its test binary compiled for
        // aarch64-macos and x86_64-macos, where fe5a07b's kqueue path lives.
        // On this side, `zoxy --check` prices the example config and a TLS
        // one byte-for-byte as v0.9.0 does, so no §5 number moves.
        //
        // Re-audit for 010be2a, signed off 2026-08-27. `Completion.connect`
        // carries a `net.Address`, whose union members top out at 28 bytes;
        // a `sockaddr_un` is 110, and `getOsSockLen`'s catch-all arm hands
        // the kernel `@sizeOf(sockaddr)` — so an AF_UNIX destination was
        // not merely unimplemented, it was inexpressible, and a path would
        // have been truncated to thirteen characters. Both backends prep
        // from `&v.addr.any` and that length, so the fix has to be in the
        // union rather than at either call site.
        //
        // The diff is three things: a `un` member, an `initUnix` that
        // rejects a path with no room for its terminator (and rejects the
        // empty path, i.e. the abstract namespace, whose length is the
        // caller's to state rather than the constructor's to derive), and
        // an `AF.UNIX` arm deriving the length from that terminator as
        // `unix(7)` documents. Nothing existing changes shape: the IP arms
        // of `getOsSockLen`, `toIpAddress` and `fromIpAddress` are
        // untouched, and no backend gained an op. `TCPStream` grows
        // `initAddress` and `connectAddress` — the same socket setup and
        // op construction, entered with an address already in posix form
        // — and `init`/`connect` become one-line delegations, so their
        // `std.Io.net.IpAddress` surface is unchanged. `TCPDynamic` is
        // untouched; nothing that can name a `net.Address` reaches it.
        // `main.zig` re-exports the `net` namespace those two take, which
        // is the only way a caller can name a family `std.Io.net` has no
        // spelling for.
        //
        // What it costs, stated because §5 is closed-form: the kernel reads
        // the address from one contiguous location that must outlive the
        // submission, and the completion is the only storage the op has, so
        // the path is inline and `Completion` grows 128 -> 152 bytes
        // (io_uring) and 184 -> 208 (epoll). zoxy holds two per conn and
        // four per stream, so the effective config's arena grows by
        // 144 B x conn slots — 0.4% of the banner's total at the shipped
        // shape, against relay buffers that dominate it. Upstream's two
        // size-guard tests move with the number rather than being deleted;
        // being told about exactly this is what they are for.
        //
        // Re-audit for fe5a07b, signed off 2026-08-27. **kqueue only**, and
        // the one commit in this stack that fixes upstream rather than
        // extending it: `Loop.submit` was completing readiness reported to
        // its own kevent, whose eventlist aliases its changelist. Safe with
        // one call, wrong with two — a submission queue longer than
        // `events.len` is drained in batches, and a completion batch 1
        // registered comes back ready in batch 2's reply, already performed
        // and already linked into `completions`. The second push splices a
        // linked node and the two intrusive lists cross; `queue.push`'s
        // assert catches it in ReleaseSafe, and ReleaseFast spins on the
        // cycle forever — a core pinned, both listeners dead, SIGTERM never
        // delivered because the loop never returns to the kernel (#314).
        //
        // The diff is one `continue` and its reasoning: readiness belongs to
        // the wait, which already owns it (the wait has always ignored
        // EV_ERROR, the mirror of this split). Every filter here is
        // level-triggered, so nothing is lost by leaving it — except
        // EVFILT_MACHPORT, which arms with MACH_RCV_MSG and so *delivers* on
        // report rather than signalling; that one is still consumed, and the
        // draft that skipped it hung the async tests. No API, error set, or
        // io_uring path is touched.
        //
        // Upstream has this as mitchellh/libxev#122 (2024) and #169 (2025),
        // both open, both load-level repros with no mechanism named; #227 and
        // #234 are the IOCP and epoll members of the family. mitchellh/libxev#224
        // clears the same assert with `c.next = null` before the push, which
        // truncates whichever list the node was linked in and silently orphans
        // the rest — the reason this carries its own fix rather than that one.
        // Send upstream; the pin moves only after re-audit either way.
        //
        // Re-audit for 24da6d0 (§4, §5's head-buffer ring), signed off
        // 2026-08-02 against zoxy-io/libxev#3 (standing open like the rest
        // of the stack — the PR is the audit vehicle, the pin is the
        // record). One backend op
        // (`recv_group`: prep mirrors std's buffer_selection recv arm), the
        // raw cqe flags kept on `Completion` beside `result_errno` with a
        // `bufferId` reader, and ENOBUFS surviving the recv mapping as
        // `error.NoBuffers`. io_uring only; no watcher or dynamic-API
        // surface (a new op, not a new ReadBuffer arm, so the shared
        // exhaustive switches stay untouched); registration and buffer
        // returns stay caller-side via std against `loop.ring`. The test
        // pins selection, exhaustion, syscall-free reuse, and that EOF
        // consumes no buffer — the fact §5's no-leak-at-teardown rests on.
        //
        // Re-audit for b3d6b55: one `posix.E` field on `Completion`
        // defaulting to `.SUCCESS`, one assignment at the top of `invoke`
        // from the same `-errno` encoding every arm already decodes, and a
        // test. No error set, result, or control-flow change — the whole
        // diff is additive, so every existing call path behaves as before.
        // Scope: **io_uring only**. `kqueue.Completion` has no such field,
        // so `XevIo.recordPressure` reports unclassified there rather than
        // failing to compile on the macOS dev box (§4).
        // It exists because the mapping's `else => posix.unexpectedErrno`
        // arms discard which errno failed, which is what left a c10k run
        // reporting 227,628 indistinguishable failures.
        .libxev = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zoxy-io/libxev#b0268b106fe4a1d274d759581d821f8490a40978",
            .hash = "libxev-0.0.0-86vtc2l2FACqC0P-gGqU0h_zG2B2WYWGtILfBGq5lUGt",
        },
        // Load generator, §9 Tier 0/1 only — never linked into the proxy
        // (no `src/` file imports it), so a bump moves what the benchmark
        // measures *with*, not what it measures. Held at the same version
        // the cloud benchmark runs, so a local band and a fleet number
        // come off the same generator; runs on mismatched versions are not
        // comparable, which is a trap this pin exists to close.
        //
        // **A tag, v3.0.1, and still an exception to the paragraph
        // above.** The comparability rule wants this pin to match
        // zoxy-io/benchmark's, and it does not: the benchmark repo runs
        // v2.4.2. So a local band measured today is **not comparable to a
        // fleet number** until that repo moves. Read the nightly report,
        // not a local figure, while this says so.
        //
        // It moved because it had to: no 2.x builds on Zig 0.17, and
        // v3.0.1 is the first release that does (zoxy-io/zrk#99). It is a
        // major version — HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 clients, wrk-style Lua scripts
        // on LuaJIT, a Workload interface — and none of that is reached
        // from here. bench/run.zig and bench/profile.zig compiled against
        // it unchanged, and every field 3.0 added to `cli.Config` defaults
        // off (`http2`, `http3`, `workload` and `script_path` null,
        // `streams` 1), so the bands are still HTTP/1.1 from the same
        // runner. What does reach here is LuaJIT's build: C, compiled into
        // both harnesses. A C surface in the *measurement* binaries, never
        // the proxy — no `src/` file imports zrk.
        //
        // The zssl gap the previous record left open is closed: v3.0.1
        // pins zssl 827c7e5, the commit this file pins, and both reach the
        // one zoxy-io/openssl 0377b65. The optimize-mode collision that
        // keeps `zoxy-profile-harness` off the `zoxy` module (see
        // build.zig) is unaffected and still reason enough on its own.
        //
        // The zio bump beside it is not optional. zrk carries zio 0.20.1 at
        // c0a6b58, and the rule below — that this file's zio must be
        // *exactly* zrk's — is what keeps the harness and zrk's runner on
        // one runtime.
        .zrk = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zoxy-io/zrk?ref=v3.0.1#518e5d2f2a2da91d148100312722d9ee6ce76667",
            .hash = "zrk-3.0.1-WqdFOyTkDQCqDocn7xp4MLGWm_lQOzlEExpy41dEUBio",
            // Lazy, and only asked for under -Dbench (the default): see
            // `addLoadGenerator` in build.zig for why both halves are needed.
            .lazy = true,
        },
        // zrk embeds its load off a zio (io_uring) runtime, not the default
        // std.Io.Threaded backend — plain Threaded is missing pieces zrk
        // calls (e.g. connect-with-timeout is still a TODO stub there). The
        // bench harness constructs its own zio.Runtime to match (see
        // bench/run.zig), so this pin must stay *exactly* the one zrk's own
        // build.zig.zon carries: two zio copies would be two runtimes, and
        // zrk's runner would be handed an `Io` from the wrong one. Upstream
        // rather than a fork, deliberately — a fork of a dependency we do
        // not patch is a second thing to keep in sync with zrk's pin, and
        // the only way it can differ from upstream is by being wrong.
        //
        // c0a6b58 (v0.20.1) is what zrk v3.0.1 carries — which is the only
        // reason this line has that value. It moved with the zrk bump above
        // and for no other reason; the lockstep rule is the whole content of
        // this pin.
        //
        // It is 202 commits ahead of the 3820667 this used to pin, and zero
        // behind (`compare/3820667...c0a6b58`). That one was itself 121
        // ahead of b822fda and zero behind, so the fix b822fda was pinned
        // for is still contained rather than lost: zio#628, "flush a full
        // submission queue instead of dropping SQEs". Before it, zio dropped a cancel when the io_uring SQ was
        // full, leaving the target completion stuck `.running` forever and the
        // awaiting task blocked with it — which a c10k load reaches reliably,
        // since ten thousand per-operation timeouts expiring together submit
        // thousands of cancels at once (lalinsky/zio#623). Checked with
        // `compare/b822fda...df666c2` before moving, because "the fix is still
        // in there" is exactly the assumption a bump is where you lose.
        .zio = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/lalinsky/zio?ref=v0.20.1#c0a6b5877c9bbc71d2d2c0b3374ce7fa61a4ec12",
            .hash = "zio-0.20.1-xHbVVI0wLwDgE2dXWDCWH0UfCQYEhnBYHs1Td12oAGeR",
            // Lazy, and only asked for under -Dbench (the default): see
            // `addLoadGenerator` in build.zig for why both halves are needed.
            .lazy = true,
        },
        // Audited fork: zoxy-io/hparse, pinned by content hash; the pin
        // moves only after re-audit, and the paragraphs below are that
        // record.
        //
        // Re-audit for 7f57ef8 (Zig 0.17), 2026-11-13: one commit on, and no
        // verdict moves. Its whole `src/root.zig` delta is the `keys` and
        // `values` span defaults spelled `@splat(.{})` instead of
        // `** max_headers`. Everything else is harness — the fuzz target's
        // two `@cImport`s become translated modules and the vendored fuzz
        // test runner is deleted — and none of it links here. No table is
        // in the diff.
        //
        // Re-audit for 2dcaf45, six commits on and performance only. Two of
        // them never reach this build — 2f2c6cd re-measures the README, 0db3856
        // rebuilds the benchmark harness — and the other four are the whole
        // src/ delta. No table changed: checked by filtering the diff for map,
        // table and hex-byte lines, which is the standing check this pin's
        // history says to run, and the only arithmetic line it turns up keeps
        // its constants. So no verdict moves, and zoxy parses exactly the bytes
        // it parsed before. What moves is scan speed.
        //
        // The one rewrite that could have changed a verdict is
        // `matchHeaderValue`'s mask, restated as its De Morgan negation so both
        // scans hand `firstRejected` the same reject convention: the old
        // `~((chunk > 31 | chunk == TAB) & ~(chunk == DEL))` is the new
        // `~(chunk > 31 | chunk == TAB) | (chunk == DEL)`, which is exact.
        // Both still describe `value_map` — TAB, 0x20-0x7e, 0x80-0xff — and
        // that table is byte-identical.
        //
        // dee665b is the shape change. Both vector loops used to advance by
        // `@ctz(mask)` every iteration, so the next load's address waited on the
        // whole classify/mask/count chain even on a clean chunk, where the
        // answer is always `vec_size`. They now `@reduce(.Or, bad)` first and
        // advance by a literal, asking for a position only on the chunk that
        // stops the scan. Same bytes consumed, off the loop-carried dependency.
        //
        // 416ca0a is the one that matters most HERE, because it is aarch64-only
        // and release.yml ships aarch64-linux and aarch64-macos. x86 keeps the
        // bit-per-lane `pmovmskb` form byte-for-byte, so the tier this dev box
        // and the x86 artifacts run is unchanged; aarch64 gets a `shrn` nibble
        // mask instead of the `and`/`ext`/`zip1`/`addv`/`fmov` reduction LLVM
        // was emitting inside the dependency chain. Verified the packing by
        // hand rather than trusting the measurement: narrowing each u16 lane
        // right by four leaves the even byte's high nibble in the low half and
        // the odd byte's low nibble in the high half, so a rejected lane sets
        // its nibble to 0xf and `@ctz >> 2` is the lane index. That reading is
        // little-endian-only, and `is_aarch64` excludes `.aarch64_be`
        // deliberately, leaving a big-endian build on the path it already took.
        //
        // 2dcaf45 adds `scalar_prefix = 2`: `matchHeaderValue` classifies two
        // bytes scalar before entering the vector loop. The gate there is
        // `hasLength(vec_size)`, which asks how much BUFFER remains and not how
        // long the VALUE is, so a one-byte value mid-head paid a full chunk to
        // find a CR one byte away. The prefix uses `isValidValueChar`, the same
        // classifier as the tail, and returns on the first invalid byte, so it
        // cannot admit or consume anything the vector tier would not have. Its
        // worst case is 3% (`chrome`, a 2013 capture with no short values);
        // `many-tiny` is 2.12x. `matchPath` pointedly does NOT get one, and the
        // fork measured why — 12% worse on a long target, since a target is
        // only ever one or two bytes for `GET /`.
        //
        // d2e0cc3 drops two unused imports and is inert.
        //
        // The asserts the new `firstRejected` carries are Debug and ReleaseSafe
        // only, which is the build release.yml ships (#283) — so the claim that
        // the reduction and the mask agree about the same chunk is checked in
        // the artifact, not just in the fork's tests.
        //
        // Re-audit for 11e4735, one commit on and performance only. The
        // entire code delta is two deletions: `matchHeaderValue`'s middle
        // SWAR tier, and `broadcast()`, which had no callers left after it.
        // No table changes — checked by filtering comments out of the diff,
        // because a table edit hiding inside a commit that says "perf" is
        // the one thing this pin's history says to look for. So no verdict
        // moves, and the two tests added here for tchar and pchar need no
        // companion.
        //
        // Deleting a tier is faster in both builds: 0.99x with vectors on,
        // 0.70x under `-Duse-vectors=false` where it did all the work. Ours
        // is the vectors-on figure, so this is worth about 1% on header
        // values — taken because it removes 30 lines of bit arithmetic
        // carrying a borrow hazard and a tab special case, and one fewer
        // tier is one fewer place the tiers can disagree.
        //
        // Why it never paid: with vectors on it only ever saw the
        // 8-to-31-byte window at the tail of the BUFFER (its loop bound is
        // bytes remaining in the buffer, not in the value), and it could
        // not express "TAB is legal" in the arithmetic — a borrow from a
        // genuine sub-0x20 byte perturbs the next byte's high bit — so it
        // restarted on every tab where the table lookup just says yes.
        //
        // It also records that `status_msg_map` rejects HTAB, which RFC
        // 9112 §4 permits in a reason-phrase: a deliberate deviation in the
        // strict direction, previously silent. Documentation, not a change
        // — the table is byte-identical. zoxy never reads a reason-phrase's
        // bytes, so nothing here depends on it either way.
        //
        // Two commits on from 554d9fb, and only one of them is product:
        // 4c670e6 gives CI's test-runner watchdog room on a cold cache and
        // touches no file that links here.
        //
        // Re-audit for 0c8060a, which is why this pin is at 0c8060a and not
        // the 554d9fb it sat at for one afternoon. `path_map` had the same
        // denylist shape `key_map` did — {controls, space, DEL} — so a
        // request-target could carry `"<>\^`{|}` and every byte from 0x80
        // up, none of which RFC 3986 admits unencoded. It is a positive
        // pchar table now, built at comptime from the grammar's own pieces.
        //
        // The one that makes this a security fix rather than a conformance
        // one is `\`. IIS, .NET and browser URL parsing normalize a
        // backslash to `/`, so zoxy routing on `/public\..\admin` and an
        // origin resolving `/admin` have read two different paths out of
        // one target — and §7 routes on paths, which is the fact that
        // scoped the fix. Verified against this tree before taking the pin:
        // at 554d9fb that target parsed and reached routing with the
        // backslash intact, because `validateTarget` checks the target's
        // *form* and never its character class. NOT introduced by 554d9fb;
        // v0.6.2 ships it, which is the argument for moving rather than
        // holding.
        //
        // Rejecting 0x80-0xff is the part with an operational cost, and it
        // is deliberate: raw UTF-8 in a path is common in the wild despite
        // RFC 3986, and those requests now draw 400. `#`, `[` and `]` are
        // tolerated against the letter of the grammar — a fragment fails
        // SAFE (an origin truncating there sees less path than the proxy
        // did, never more) and brackets are everywhere in query strings.
        //
        // `matchPath` KEEPS its vector tier, which is the opposite of the
        // call `matchHeaderKey` made two commits earlier, and the
        // difference is length: both regimes were measured, and
        // scalar-only came out 1.03x on an 8-byte target but 1.30x on a
        // 165-byte one, because a target with a query string amortizes a
        // 16-byte setup where a field name never does. Stating the set as
        // a REJECT mask then landed at 0.99x — one wrapping subtract and
        // one compare catch controls, space, DEL and all of 0x80-0xff
        // together, and feed `@ctz` with no inversion. The SWAR tier is
        // deleted: it expressed the same range compare in integer
        // arithmetic, and pchar is not a range.
        //
        // What this does NOT buy: safe path routing. The dominant
        // confusions are percent-encoding and dot-segments, and hparse
        // decodes neither — `canonicalTarget` still owns normalization
        // (§7), and the fork's README now says so from its side. What the
        // table buys is that the proxy and the origin cannot read
        // *different bytes* as different paths.
        //
        // Re-audit for 554d9fb, which this passed through: nine commits on
        // from 87fd39d. Seven of them are harness — an llhttp differential
        // and its imported corpus, a picohttpparser differential, a
        // disagreement ledger, a copyright line, a contributor guide — and
        // touch no file that links into zoxy. Two reach `src/root.zig`, the
        // parser itself, and only one of those changes a verdict.
        //
        // b65ea98 is the correctness fix and the reason to take this pin.
        // `key_map` was written as a denylist — controls, space, colon, DEL
        // — so `isValidKeyChar` accepted 144 bytes that are not tchar: the
        // RFC 9110 §5.6.2 separators `"(),/;<=>?@[\]{}` and every byte
        // 0x80-0xff. A field name is `token`, the same grammar as a method,
        // and `method_map` in the same file had been the correct allowlist
        // all along; `key_map` is now literally that table.
        //
        // Unlike the empty-target fix this pin used to carry, this one DOES
        // change behaviour here. §7's wrapper owns strictness for the
        // target, the method and the framing, but it never inspected a
        // field name — the key arrived from hparse already judged. So a
        // request whose header name carries a separator or a high byte now
        // draws 400 where it used to be forwarded verbatim. That is the
        // point: handing a backend a field name the backend tokenizes
        // differently is the smuggling shape §7's CRLF-only rule already
        // exists for, and a denylist is how it got in.
        //
        // The same commit deletes `matchHeaderKey`'s vector path rather
        // than fixing it — the `chunk > ' '` range compare is precisely
        // what admitted the 144 — after measuring an exact tchar classifier
        // at 1.24x SLOWER than the broken code. Header keys are short
        // enough that a 16-byte setup never amortizes, so the byte-at-a-time
        // loop that replaces both is 0.85x: correct and faster, which is
        // the "performance" half of this bump.
        //
        // 8625977 turns `use_vectors` and `vec_size` from hardcoded
        // constants into `?bool` / `?u16` build options, so the fork can
        // fuzz a forced-scalar build against the vector one. This build
        // passes neither, and unset they compute exactly what the constants
        // computed, so the scan tier zoxy ships is unchanged. Its one
        // visible consequence is that hparse's root now imports
        // `build_options`, which its own build.zig supplies — nothing to
        // wire on this side.
        //
        // Still NOT in use here: hparse's resume API, which the 87fd39d pin
        // brought and this one leaves alone — `HeadCursor` scans for the
        // terminator and one-shot parses a head already known to be
        // complete, and the comment there says why.
        .hparse = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zoxy-io/hparse#7f57ef8183f15ebd62f5f9ca631f69a9afdbc421",
            .hash = "hparse-0.2.0-IukW0jqfAwDmg7ZWyJSUjHGaocYJuqDaNIo8A9URJOjo",
        },
        // libcrypto, built by Zig rather than found on the system.
        //
        // This is what buys back cross-compilation. `linkSystemLibrary("crypto")`
        // resolved libcrypto out of the *host's* nix store, so every target but
        // the host's own failed to link — the reason release.yml builds natively
        // on one runner per target, and the reason x86_64-macos has been absent
        // since 0.2.0 for want of an Intel runner.
        //
        // OpenSSL, not BoringSSL: the BoringSSL family has no
        // `CRYPTO_set_mem_functions`, so `tls/libcrypto_heap.zig`'s `install`
        // would return false and `main.zig` would refuse to start (§5's
        // zero-allocation budget cannot be kept without it). That rules out the
        // package zrk was using and made this fork the shared answer.
        //
        // The fork carries allyourcodebase/openssl#5 — pre-generated perlasm for
        // aarch64, which upstream has only for x86_64 — on top of a bump from
        // 3.3.2 to 3.5.7. 3.5 is the LTS line, supported to April 2030. 3.6.3
        // was measured and rejected: it restructured the provider tree, leaving
        // 62 sources and 2 templates without a home against 3 for 3.5.7.
        //
        // Versus what this build linked before: nixpkgs supplies 3.6.3, so the
        // libcrypto version steps back one minor release onto a supported LTS
        // branch, and forward two years from the package's own 3.3.2.
        .openssl = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zoxy-io/openssl?ref=main#0377b65001a49a33e5fd69f2ad561f0f9dcb0d69",
            .hash = "openssl-3.5.7-TC9C3eDUfwH9TWFR4TWTjcA8oCo_VrNIqg20l63i8uZF",
        },
        // zssl — the TLS 1.3 engine (DESIGN.md §4), written for this
        // proxy rather than adapted to it. It replaces the audited
        // zoxy-io/ztls fork, whose five carried commits were all fixes
        // this codebase needed and upstream had not taken: deterministic
        // ECDSA nonces for §9's replay oracle, libcrypto allocation
        // hooks for §5's budget, a bounded teardown wipe, server-side
        // ticket issuance, and #222's keygen livelock. Maintaining that
        // delta against a pre-alpha upstream was the cost of borrowing;
        // owning the protocol layer removes it.
        //
        // What zoxy gains beyond parity: kTLS key export as first-class
        // state (the §4 follow-up that ztls had no seam for), RFC 8446
        // §5's length caps enforced at header parse rather than
        // compensated for in `Engine.pump`, and a client handshake for
        // the upstream origination §7's cluster `tls` block describes.
        //
        // What it costs: this tree is now the audit surface. zssl's own
        // gates are the evidence, and the paragraphs below are the
        // record of reading them.
        //
        // Re-audit for 827c7e5 (Zig 0.17), 2026-11-13 — the commit zrk
        // v3.0.1 pins, which puts the two back on one zssl (see zrk
        // above). Six commits on from 91a985a, read for what reaches zoxy:
        //
        //   - 827c7e5 is the port. zssl's one `@cImport` became a
        //     translated module, `libcrypto`, which zssl's build.zig makes
        //     from the headers of the same zoxy-io/openssl pin this file
        //     carries — one libcrypto still, and now one header tree. The
        //     rest is `**`, reflection and builtin renames.
        //   - a9f5638 is the one on zoxy's serving path: the server's
        //     `awaiting_finished` splits into WAIT_CERT, WAIT_CV and
        //     WAIT_FINISHED (#18). zoxy sets no `client_auth`, so
        //     `afterServerFlight` answers `.awaiting_finished` exactly as
        //     the assignment it replaced did, and `awaitingClientFlight()`
        //     admits the states the old switch listed. No alert moves.
        //   - d2ee876, f249ad9 and c68966b are the client's: Appendix
        //     A.1's flight order in the state type, a bare Finished
        //     standing in for EncryptedExtensions refused, and an empty
        //     ticket nonce accepted. zoxy reaches `ClientHandshake` only
        //     from tests — `TestClient`, the engine and heap-proof tests —
        //     and they pass.
        //   - 911ed54 is bench prose.
        //
        // What it costs: nothing. `zoxy --check` on a TLS config prices
        // `tls engines 1024 x 94448 B`, the line v0.9.0 prints, so
        // `@sizeOf(Engine)` and §5's ~92 KiB stand.
        //
        // Re-audit for 91a985a, signed off 2026-08-31, and the largest
        // single move this pin will ever make: ~130 commits, +11k lines
        // under `src/`. The 992ee9d record ended by saying docs/BOGO.md
        // "names the adversarial gate still missing" — that sentence is
        // what moved. BoGo runs (`zig build bogo`: 454 passing against a
        // floor, 6490 declined by the shim, 13 suppressed on a ledger),
        // and two more adversarial oracles arrived beside it: tlsfuzzer
        // driving our server (22 of 62 scripts) and TLS-Anvil's
        // RFC-derived corpus (115 passing, 321 declined as DTLS, QUIC or
        // pre-1.3). Each holds a counted floor, so a quiet suppression
        // fails the build rather than reading as a pass.
        //
        // The reason to take the pin is what those three found, and the
        // parts of it that land on the path zoxy actually serves:
        //
        //   - `flood.zig`: 32 consecutive empty records, 32 KeyUpdates,
        //     4 warning alerts, then the connection ends. All three are
        //     legal records that cost a decryption and return nothing —
        //     RFC 8446 caps none of them, so before this a peer could
        //     buy unbounded work per connection for free. Counters reset
        //     on progress, so real traffic never approaches them.
        //   - `protect`: RFC 8446 §5.4's cap on the *inner* plaintext,
        //     enforced rather than compensated for downstream.
        //   - `record`: a length the message type cannot have is
        //     refused, a record refused at its header now draws the
        //     alert it owes, and the ChangeCipherSpec payload is read
        //     instead of assumed (a hundred 0x01s in one record used to
        //     pass as filler).
        //   - `der_bounds`: `std.crypto.Certificate.parse` computes the
        //     next element's offset from the last one's end and reads
        //     there unchecked, so seven bytes of hostile DER *panic*
        //     rather than erroring — a remote abort in ReleaseSafe,
        //     which is the build release.yml ships (#283). A framing
        //     pre-pass now runs first. Not on zoxy's serving path today
        //     (no client certificates are asked for), but it is on the
        //     client handshake §7's cluster `tls` block will use.
        //
        // Five API changes had to be taken here, and each is a decision
        // rather than a rename:
        //
        //   1. `handleRecord` answers `?Event` and `drain` returns the
        //      rest — one record may legally carry several
        //      post-handshake messages, and a caller that stops early is
        //      told `error.EventsPending` on the *next* record instead
        //      of silently losing one. `Engine.pump` is that loop now,
        //      and the outbox room `received` demands moved with it:
        //      from one control record to the KeyUpdate burst the flood
        //      guard bounds, which is a closed form only because the
        //      guard exists.
        //   2. `x25519_private` became `key_share_private`, sized for
        //      secp384r1, beside a `groups` list defaulting to all three
        //      groups zssl can now complete server-side. zoxy pins
        //      x25519 alone: `acquireTlsEngine` draws 32 bytes of seed,
        //      a P-384 scalar is 48, and widening the offer is an
        //      entropy-draw decision of its own rather than a
        //      consequence of a bump.
        //   3. `PskLookup` answers a `Psk` — length, kind, optional
        //      issuance and early-data terms — not a length. zoxy
        //      answers `.resumption` with both optionals null: RFC 8446
        //      §4.6.1's lifetime stays enforced against the sealed stamp
        //      the client cannot move, and a ticket that permits no
        //      early data is one of the three opt-ins 0-RTT needs.
        //   4. `Event` gained `.early_data`, deliberately as its own arm
        //      so an embedder has to answer for it. zoxy refuses it: no
        //      clock, no strike register, no ticket terms, so accepted
        //      0-RTT cannot arrive, and if a later pin makes it possible
        //      the connection dies rather than the proxy relaying bytes
        //      that are neither forward secret nor replay-proof.
        //   5. **The one that changes behaviour without changing a call
        //      site.** `backend.Signer` learned RSA-PSS, so
        //      `error.UnsupportedKey` no longer refuses an RSA leaf —
        //      and zoxy's ECDSA-only promise (an RSA sign is ~1-2 ms
        //      against the ~260 µs handshake the single loop is sized
        //      for) would have left with this bump. It is
        //      `tls/Credentials.zig`'s own rule now, with its own test
        //      and its own fixture.
        //
        // What it costs, stated because §5 is closed-form: the server
        // machine grows 1248 -> 2128 bytes, so `@sizeOf(Engine)` goes
        // 93,568 -> 94,448 — the ~91 KiB in the banner, DESIGN.md §5's
        // table and four doc comments is ~92 KiB now, and
        // `tls_engine_bytes_max` (160 KiB) is untouched. Against that,
        // the server does one scalar multiplication per handshake
        // instead of two: `EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key` leaves the
        // public half absent and OpenSSL derives it on import, so
        // deriving the share and then agreeing multiplied twice. zssl's
        // own bench puts a full handshake at 175 µs where it was 495;
        // most of that is a client-side verify move, but the key-share
        // half is ours.
        //
        // Newly available and deliberately not taken, each its own
        // slice: server-side secp256r1/secp384r1 (see 2 above), mTLS via
        // RFC 8446 §4.3.2's CertificateRequest, RFC 5705 exporters, and
        // 0-RTT with the strike register RFC 8446 §8.2 needs.
        .zssl = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zoxy-io/zssl#827c7e5002aef45cc2a14f99200bdf288f4a0f0c",
            .hash = "zssl-0.0.1-5ocyiJXbEACoixJ3negjZSa8wikXraZUW4VPpKKa_5bO",
        },
    },
    .paths = .{
        "build.zig",
        "build.zig.zon",
        "src",
        // For example...
        //"LICENSE",
        //"README.md",
    },
}
